:: GrandmotherPlants [childhood]
# Wisdom of the Earth

Your grandmother takes you by the hand and leads you to the edge of camp. "Today we will learn about the plants that feed us," she says.

She points out different plants growing nearby, naming each one and explaining its uses. Some are good for food, others for medicine, some for making tools or baskets, and a few that must be avoided because they are poisonous.

"See these leaves? They have five points, like your hand. These are safe to eat and taste sweet in the spring." She picks a few and gives you one to taste.

"But these," she points to a similar plant with slightly different leaves, "will make your stomach hurt badly. See how the edges of the leaves are different? You must learn to tell them apart."

Your grandmother's knowledge is vast, gained from a lifetime of gathering and using plants. She explains that women are often the keepers of this knowledge, passing it from mother to daughter through many generations.

